---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buyerfinderUrl, siteUrl } from "@/lib/links";
import { contactUrl } from "@/lib/present";
import { mailReady } from "@/lib/site";
import { callsPerDay } from "@/mcp/http.ts";

const mcpUrl = `${siteUrl}/mcp`;
---

<Layout
  title="How BuyerFinder Radar works"
  description="How Radar finds public posts where people ask which product to buy, what it shows, how to search it from Claude or ChatGPT, and how to have a post removed."
  image="/og/about.png"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-4 pt-12 sm:px-8">
    <p class="text-[0.8125rem] font-semibold tracking-wider text-primary uppercase">How it works</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] sm:text-4xl">What Radar does</h1>

    <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      <p>
        Every hour Radar searches Reddit and X for public posts with phrases like
        <em>"can anyone recommend"</em>, <em>"what do you use"</em> and <em>"is it worth buying"</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        A simple text rule sets most of them aside for free. An AI model reads the rest and answers
        two questions: does the author ask which product to use or buy, and what kind of product is it?
        It also writes one line that says what the person wants.
      </p>
      <p>
        Radar is made by{" "}
        <a href={buyerfinderUrl("about")} class="font-semibold text-primary hover:underline">BuyerFinder</a>.
        BuyerFinder does the same job for one product: paste your website, and it shows you the posts
        where people ask for a product like yours.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What Radar shows, and what it does not</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      <li>A short excerpt of each post and a link to the original.</li>
      <li>No usernames, no profile pictures, and no copy of the full post.</li>
      <li>Pages show the last 30 days. Through <a href="#mcp" class="font-semibold text-primary hover:underline">MCP</a>, your AI can search older posts too.</li>
    </ul>

    {mailReady() && (
      <Fragment>
        <h2 id="email" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The daily email</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
          <a href="/subscribe" class="font-semibold text-primary hover:underline">Pick tags</a> for what you sell, and
          every morning we email you the new posts under them. We keep your email address, your tags, and when you
          confirmed: nothing else. Every email has a link to change your tags and one to stop the emails.
        </p>
      </Fragment>
    )}

    <h2 id="mcp" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Ask your own AI</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      Radar is also an MCP server, so Claude or ChatGPT can search it for you: "what CRMs are people leaving this
      month?", "find posts asking for a standing desk under $400". It is free and needs no sign-in. Add this URL as a
      custom connector:
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border bg-card px-4 py-3 font-mono text-[0.9375rem] break-all text-foreground">{mcpUrl}</p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      <li><strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">Claude:</strong> Settings, Connectors, add a custom connector, paste the URL.</li>
      <li>
        <strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">ChatGPT</strong> (Plus or Pro): turn on Developer mode under
        Settings, Connectors, Advanced. Then create a connector with the URL and no authentication.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      Your AI sees what the pages show, excerpts and links with no usernames, but it can search every post Radar
      has kept, not only the last 30 days. One address can make {callsPerDay} calls a day. The menu names in both
      apps change from time to time.
    </p>

    <h2 id="remove" class="mt-12 scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Remove a post</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-secondary-foreground">
      If a post on Radar is yours, or you want it gone for any other reason,{" "}
      <a href={contactUrl} class="font-semibold text-primary hover:underline">send us its link</a>.
      We remove it, and Radar will not show it again.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
